1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1726 — A parent declared *after* its child is resolved as a valid `extends` target, diverging from the runtime. Trace: `pending_parents` records `(child, index+3)` at src/lsp.rs:1191-1200; the deferred pass attaches any binding the later name got at src/lsp.rs:1722-1730; `nearest_object_member`/`member_binding` (src/lsp.rs:836-881) then resolves inherited members. But `Interpreter::declare_object` resolves the chain at declaration time and errors for an undeclared parent: `"Object '{name}' extends '{current}', which is not declared"` (src/interpreter.rs:3002-3006). So `goto_definition`/`find_references` answer on `object Employee extends Person` / `object Person` written below, while `rb run` refuses to execute that program — and the report justifies this as matching the runtime. Fix: attach only parents already in scope at the `object` token (drop `pending_parents`), or explicitly document forward-parent leniency as a deliberate divergence and remove the "runtime agrees" claim. VERIFIED: traced the pending-parent → deferred-attach path and quoted src/interpreter.rs:3002-3006.

2. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:3019 — `edge_extends_cycle_does_not_hang_member_navigation` is not a runnable program: `object A extends B` with `B` declared below is rejected by the interpreter (`... which is not declared`, src/interpreter.rs:3004) before any chain walk, so it does not pin a reachable cycle; it only exercises the LSP's defensive `seen` guard on an invalid forward reference. Missing tests: a valid parent-above-child multi-level chain (`Child` → `Parent` → `GrandParent` field) and a fixture pinning the chosen forward-parent behaviour. Fix: add those, and keep the cycle fixture only if forward resolution is intentionally kept.

